---
/**
 * Port of casestudy/pepsi.html. Two source bugs the plan calls out are moot
 * here rather than fixed in place: the stray `<<script` and the duplicate
 * `</section>` were HTML-parser artifacts of literal markup — this page is
 * authored as valid JSX from scratch, and BaseLayout now owns the one
 * shared reveal script, so there's no per-page script tag or malformed
 * closing tag to carry over.
 *
 * Process combines the source's two sibling `.content-text` sections
 * ("Supply chain management flow" + "Customer research") into one
 * ContentBlock: as two literal siblings under one `.content-title` they'd
 * grid-wrap under column 1 instead of stacking in column 2 (only a 2-column
 * grid), which is a source authoring bug, not intended layout.
 *
 * The three ~120px persona photos (manager/worker/delivery.png) have no
 * source CSS class at all — plain unstyled `<img>`, ported as-is.
 * `worker.mp4` is a portrait clip (478x956); source constrained it to
 * `width="280px "` (the plan's named bug) instead of the other two GIFs'
 * 100%, so it isn't overwhelmingly tall — ported via a local max-width
 * wrapper (single-use, scoped here, same precedent as market.astro's
 * `.solution-image-grid`) instead of stretching it full-width like
 * `ContentMedia` normally would.
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../../components/Eyebrow.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="PepsiCo — Jury Alyavdin"
  description="Designing a responsive supply-chain platform that helps PepsiCo warehouse and delivery teams collect, transport, and deliver goods more efficiently."
  path="/casestudy/pepsi.html"
  h1="Supply chain management service"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/pepsi/pepsi-logo.png"
  orbitTop="PepsiCo"
  orbitBottom="SaaS • Food company"
  orbitTopOffset="9%"
  orbitBottomOffset="60%"
  heroImage="/casestudy/pepsi/hero.png"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'Web' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Product Designer' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2021' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      PepsiCo, Inc. is a portion of American multinational food, snack, and beverage corporation headquartered in
      Harrison, New York, in Purchase hamlet. PepsiCo products are enjoyed by consumers more than one billion times
      a day in more than 200 countries and territories worldwide. PepsiCo generated $79 billion in net revenue in
      2021. It oversees the manufacturing, distribution, and marketing of its products.
    </p>
    <p>
      This company exemplifies a perfect demand-driven supply chain strategy appropriate for the current market.
      The market where this firm operates is highly competitive. Supply chain management is handling the entire
      production flow of a good or service — starting from the raw components to delivering the final product to
      the consumer.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Goal">
    <p>
      PepsiCo recently got budget approval to work on a solution that helps supply chain teams to easily collect,
      transport and deliver finished goods from its distribution centres to end customer facilities — small shops,
      supermarkets, restaurants, etc.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project's main goal was to design a concept of a responsive product that minimizes costs by optimizing
      the production circle and helps employees achieve their goals during the supply chain management process.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Process">
    <Eyebrow as="h3">Supply chain management flow</Eyebrow>
    <p>
      Supply chain management represents an effort by suppliers to develop and implement supply chains that are as
      efficient and economical as possible. Supply chains cover everything from production to product development
      to the information systems needed to direct these undertakings.
    </p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/pepsi/flow.png" alt="Supply chain management flow diagram" width={1520} height={848} />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-section)`} />

    <Eyebrow as="h3">Customer research</Eyebrow>
    <p>
      My focus of this project was to cover the already existing process, starting from manufacturing to retail.
      It meant that I had to consider the whole worker flow from the moment they received information about the
      items to ship to the end of the delivery process. And for investigating the process I conducted a several
      interviews with warehouse employees and people related to the delivery process.
    </p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/pepsi/scenario.png" alt="Customer research scenarios across the delivery process" width={2320} height={1306} />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Solution">
    <img src="/casestudy/pepsi/manager.png" alt="Warehouse manager persona" width={120} height={120} />
    <Eyebrow as="h3">Warehouse manager</Eyebrow>
    <ul class="circle-list">
      <li>As a warehouse manager, when I get a new request from a client, I want to easily organize products that need to be shipped, so I can process more orders and receive a bonus.</li>
      <li>As a warehouse manager, when I need to pass the work for the collection stage, I want to plan the distribution and delivery of the finished goods, so the warehouse worker will not come back to me with questions and waste my time.</li>
    </ul>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/pepsi/manager"
      alt="Warehouse manager organizing and dispatching orders"
      width={960}
      height={720}
    />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-section)`} />

    <img src="/casestudy/pepsi/worker.png" alt="Warehouse worker persona" width={120} height={120} />
    <Eyebrow as="h3">Warehouse worker</Eyebrow>
    <ul class="circle-list">
      <li>As a warehouse worker, when I get information about items to collect, I want to also get information about the location of each item in the warehouse, so don't waste my time and reduce the chance to take the wrong box.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="worker-media">
      <ContentMedia
        kind="video"
        src="/casestudy/pepsi/worker"
        alt="Warehouse worker locating items via the mobile app"
        width={478}
        height={956}
      />
    </div>

    <div style={`height: var(--space-section)`} />

    <img src="/casestudy/pepsi/delivery.png" alt="Delivery manager persona" width={120} height={120} />
    <Eyebrow as="h3">Delivery manager</Eyebrow>
    <ul class="circle-list">
      <li>As a Delivery Manager, when I am managing the shipping of the order, I want to get live information on the status of orders & keep track of the products that are "on the move", so I can react immediately if something is not going according to the plan.</li>
      <li>Monitor the delivery process and get notified of any unpredicted event like challenging weather conditions, traffic jams, car accidents, etc.</li>
    </ul>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/pepsi/delivery"
      alt="Delivery manager tracking shipments in real time"
      width={800}
      height={598}
    />
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .worker-media {
    max-width: 280px;
  }
</style>
